WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Stewart Brand: I'm interested in another thing,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:07.020
Stewart Brand: you've raised the question of all
this dystopian science fiction going on.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:15.500
The so-called Golden Age of science fiction
was very upbeat, utopian...

00:00:16.162 --> 00:00:21.000
Like "Star Maker" (by Olaf Stapledon),
which is as utopian as it gets.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.840
And he was kind of a utopian socialist and whatnot.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:33.000
Generational concerns seem to have this kind of

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.060
countervailing... they're like, I want to be the mirror to

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.660
the understandings that I grew up with.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.600
The understandings that a lot of the
Golden Age folks grew up with

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.820
was a fucking World War that was truly awful.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.120
It was really devastating. Talk about dystopia.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.840
It burnt a lot of the major cities in the
world right to the ground.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.980
They lived through that.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.742
But the current dystopians didn't
grow up with anything like that.

00:01:00.742 --> 00:01:03.820
They grew up with technology getting better and better.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.520
The economics getting better and better.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.780
All the Steven Pinker world getting better and better...

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.840
Childbirth is not killing women the way it
used to do all over the world.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.540
Basically people getting out of poverty
is the main event in the world.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.300
And these guys are going
"Woah, just a goddamn minute!"

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:27.060
And then telling the counter story. Is the
counter story part of what science fiction people do?

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.340
Bruce Sterling: I actually think we have a kind of
Soviet level of dystopia.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.440
Sterling: Because if you went and hung out
in the Soviet Union

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.960
you can make the same arguments. It's like,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.760
well, you know, actually there's more roads.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.700
And like there's better buildings

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.160
And the food is improved a lot since World War II.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.060
So you know why are you actually like happy
and upbeat about being a Soviet?

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.460
In the writers union they're rewarded for that:

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.180
"I wrote a great novel about a tractor,"

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.180
It really brought in the crops. you know, it's super.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.360
But it was the lies that corroded people's morale.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.440
I think that's our actual problem now.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.020
People are dystopian because they know that the
major problems we face are being papered over.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.500
We're not dealing with the problems that we have.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.100
So there's a loss of legitimacy among institutions

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.760
and you've got people who basically are dystopian

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.240
but they're dystopian in the level of life Soviet guys mumbling black humor jokes to one another.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.780
While avoiding the situation, right?

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:32.600
And I like to make historical analogies,
but they don't always work perfectly

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:37.340
or well. And there are a lot of people in Russia right now

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.520
who are actually pretty upbeat about the situation.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.560
I mean they're proud.
They really feel like they're off their deathbed

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.760
and kind of, you know, laying out with two fists.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.580
They're ethnonationalist Russian enthusiasts.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.620
And a lot of them are Christian fundamentalists.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.580
Orthodox Christian fundamentalists.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:02.820
And they do this even though there's like a
snowstorm of official lies from the regime.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.120
I mean they just lie about everything routinely.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.420
Like guys with nerve gas in London.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.560
I mean they're lying so openly
and with such brazen lack of good faith

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.620
that it's almost like gloating.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.000
And they're actually gloating with the lies at this point.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.720
Russians used to be the most dystopian sci-fi writers

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.480
especially if they escaped Russia.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.040
Whereas now the most optimistic ones
are probably Chinese.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.060
You would not want to go to China
and say "oh what a great place!"

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:38.020
Normally you would not say 'I'm going to China for like, the better way of life, that they have.'

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:43.580
The Chinese sci-fi writers are just
punching way above their weight right now.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:48.600
This really is their Golden Age of science fiction.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.200
And they're not writing dystopian stuff, in fact
they're writing just really pretty good novels.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.260
I mean things that have emotional depth.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.120
They've got vision, they're not comic book-y or stupid.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.900
They're not serving the regime.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.700
They're really littérateurs!
You know, like they can really do it.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.340
They're in pretty good shape.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.240
So, how are you gonna explain that?

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.340
I don't know. I don't have a grand theory.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.520
But it interests me a lot that they're around.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.040
And they're not utopian.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.640
The utopia thing is overblown.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.960
People who wrote commercial science fiction
tended to write stuff with upbeat endings.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.540
Especially in the US.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.460
But the best-known works of imaginative fantasy are

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:37.480
"1984," "Brave New World," and other very serious, somber kind of geopolitical novels

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.680
that are all about oppression, torture and official lies.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:45.360
And deceit and hate and murder and phoniness.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.080
These are the things that are really
sunk down on a cultural level.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.900
You see "Orwellian" around. You see "Ballardian" around. You don't really see "Buck Rogers-ian."

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:01.940
It just seems childish.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.120
So, I don't know. It's a matter of temperament.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:13.500
Guys of my era, I mean cyberpunks,
we're into dark euphoria.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.760
We're not into a sense of wonder.
We're into ecstasy and dread.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.220
I mean ecstasy and dread is stuff that
you see a lot in cyberpunk texts.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.920
It's really horrible. But really really amazing!

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.500
And also really horrible.
But like just fantastic I can't get my head around it.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:31.920
Except his leg's been sawn off.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.700
And this is kind of a very deliberate period sensibility

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.200
that sort of emerged from the 1980s.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.360
And you could see it in film,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.060
and the way people dressed...

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.240
and that kind of thing. And, it was fun to do.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.900
Because it kind of hadn't been tried out.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:56.740
But I don't have a magic solution for it.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:01.720
And I also don't think that optimistic science fiction actually makes anybody optimistic.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:06.540
I don't think that helps with the problem at all.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.700
I mean what makes people optimistic
is something to be enthusiastic about.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:16.200
That's when people are really optimistic.
They don't even ask if they're in a good mood.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.160
They just hop out of bed because they want to go do it.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:23.480
We don't have a lot of things to be enthusiastic about.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.760
Like Moore's Law was something
or the Internet was something.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.900
But this is like your uncle's job now.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.780
We don't have something that makes people
hop out and make it happen.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.140
And if we did the oligarchs
would hit it on the head or buy it

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:43.900
or suppress in some way. And I think people know that.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.560
So that's the actual...

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:52.660
what's the right word for it? Malaise.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.240
That's the Carter-esque malaise of our era.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.400
We're not authentic.

